      "title": "Adjunct Instructor, Public Health and Rural Population Health, UC Clermont",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor, Public Health and Rural Population Health, UC Clermont Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Adjunct Instructor, Public Health and Rural Population Health, UC Clermont Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Hybrid Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors You are invited to apply to be included in the general pool of candidates from which qualified faculty roles will be filled. The number of positions varies depending on the needs of the department and there is no guarantee that an open position currently exists. Appointments are renewed based on departmental need and teaching performance. Classes may be day or evening. Rank and compensation are based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview University of Cincinnati Clermont (UCC) seeks applicants for Adjunct Faculty positions in our new Associate of Applied Science in Rural Population Health and Associate of Science in Public Health degree programs. Grounded in a population health framework, our public health programs emphasize wellness, prevention, service, and community engagement. In support of the programs’ mission and vision, and the mission of the UCC, we approach public health as the study and promotion of conditions that foster human potential and reduce barriers to optimal health and well-being. We are seeking dynamic and engaged faculty who will bring a creative, student-centered approach to classroom and online instruction. Once hired, adjunct faculty in rural population and public health may be invited to teach and develop courses as needed. On a part-time limited basis, the Adjunct Faculty member’s primary academic functions include teaching, discovering, creating, and reporting knowledge to a diverse set of students within the Public Health and Rural Population Health programs. Essential Functions Primarily responsible for the instruction, supervision, and evaluation of students in the classroom (in person or online environment) as well as course assessments for the    department. Prepare a syllabus for each course taught using the college template and requirements. Teach class in a professional manner, teaching the material that is present in the syllabus and course outline. Start and end each class on time. Communicate your teaching and student office hours schedule for each term and be present in person or virtually during student office hours. Appropriately maintain all necessary records for each course taught. Attend scheduled meetings and events as appropriate and as requested by the Program Coordinator, Department Chair or Associate Dean. Cultivate in oneself and others a commitment to helpfulness, building relationships, collaborating to find the best solution, and serving students with care and integrity. Participating in accreditation activities Participating in program recruitment efforts as requested. Other responsibilities as assigned or requested Minimum Requirements Master’s degree in public health or a bachelor's degree with extensive relevant experience. Education, Training and/or Experience A minimum of five (5) years of teaching or related professional experience is required. Required Trainings/Certifications Certified Health Education Specialist credential or Certified Doula certificate required. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities and Personal Characteristics Strong teaching, presentation, and facilitation skills. Commitment to empowering students as scholars and professionals. Exceptional writing skills and experience. Ability to work with and maintain confidential student information. Strong interpersonal skills. High degree of initiative and independent judgment. Well-developed analytical and problem-solving abilities. Strong multimedia and computer skills. Commitment to an ethic of care and service. Strong inclination to collaboration, teamwork and personal responsibility. Additional Qualifications Considered A doctoral degree in Public Health or a closely related field is preferred. Candidates with a master's degree a graduate degree in a related field with relevant coursework and/or professional experience will also be considered. Research, service or administrative experience in Public Health or a related field is preferred. Experience developing, designing, or implementing new courses is preferred. Experience in the following areas doula training, healthcare administration, epidemiology /or nutrition is preferred. Service in healthcare systems, clinics or public health organizations is highly desirable. Certified Health Education Specialist credential or CPH is preferred. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. ( UC Benefits Link ) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE. Highlights include: Tuition Remission As a part-time employee, UC provides a limited tuition remission benefit based on role.  Tuition remission covers tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS). Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time – pro-rated by FTE.  Includes paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days). Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Professional development & mentorship opportunities University employee discount program To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104372 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/AssistantAssociate-Professor-of-OperaVocal-Coaching%2C-College-Conservatory-of-Music/104383-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Vocal Coaching, College-Conservatory of Music",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Vocal Coaching, College-Conservatory of Music Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Vocal Coaching, College-Conservatory of Music Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The University of Cincinnati College-Conservatory of Music (CCM) seeks an experienced pianist/vocal coach for a tenure-track appointment within the highly collaborative Opera Department of the Division of Theatre Arts, Production and Arts Administration (TAPAA). Opera and Voice majors at CCM are predominately graduate students who have successfully completed competitive auditions for a place in one of three programs: The Artist Diploma in Opera: Vocal Performance, Coaching or Directing, the MM in Voice, and the DMA in Voice. Opera productions are supported by the Department of Theatre Design and Production, which offers a BFA/MFA in the following seven areas: Costume Design and Technology, Lighting Design and Technology, Makeup Design and Technology, Scenic Design, Sound Design, Stage Management and Technical Production. The successful candidate is expected to engage in professional activities, maintaining visibility at the regional, national and international level. Such activities may include teaching, professional engagement or creative research. It is further expected that this position should be the faculty member’s primary professional responsibility, that they will carry out their duties in person at CCM and that they will reside within the greater Cincinnati area. Rank and salary will be commensurate with credentials.  Anticipated start date: August 2027. Essential Functions Faculty Opera Coach Provide private vocal coaching to advanced (graduate-level) students in both operatic and art song literature as well as supervise or lead the musical preparation of projects within the Opera Department. Duties will be split between individual vocal coaching, the coaching of opera productions, and teaching related courses according to the needs of the Department, in addition to mentoring student pianist/coaches in their work on departmental productions. Working knowledge of lyric diction in the following languages: English, Italian, French, German. Addition languages such as Russian. Czech and Spanish are desirable. Departmental administrative responsibilities such as recruitment, attending auditions, and advising may also be part of an anticipated workload. Faculty are required to participate fully in service to the Department/Division, the College/University and the community.  Weekly department meetings, Committees, Season planning discussions and other duties as they are assigned. Minimum Requirements Prior to the effective date of the appointment, the successful candidate must have either: An advanced degree or Master's degree in music or Artists Diploma Opera Degree in addition to a minimum of five (5) years of professional experience. Such experience may include working at an institution of higher learn, or professional opera houses. OR A Bachelor's degree in music in addition to a minimum of 10 years of professional experience. Such experience may include post-baccalaureate work experience at an institution of higher learn, or professional opera houses. Additional Qualifications Considered Fluency in a second language. Experience conducting at a regional or national level. Videos to be submitted upon request. Physical Requirements/Work Environment Office environment/no specific unusual physical or environmental demands. Application Process Information Please complete an online application and include: Letter of application Curriculum vitae (CV) Teaching Statement/ Teaching Philosophy Three (3) references with their contact information. All certifications and other documentation can be uploaded using the Additional Documents section in the application. Applications without a cover letter and CV/Resume will not be considered for the position. Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104383 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Assistant%2C-Associate%2C-Full-Professor%2C-Department-of-Engineering-and-Computing-Education/104369-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor, Department of Engineering and Computing Education",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, Full Professor, Department of Engineering and Computing Education Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant, Associate, Full Professor, Department of Engineering and Computing Education Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The Department of Engineering and Computing Education (DECE) in the College of Engineering and Applied Science (CEAS) at the University of Cincinnati (UC) invites applications for multiple tenure-track positions at all ranks. Candidates will be expected to conduct research in Engineering or Computer Science (CS) Education. The program is open to diverse research areas and approaches within these fields, such as but not limited to workforce development (i.e., engineering education research with an industry focus) or P-12 education. The Department of Engineering and Computing Education was created in 2007 with the goals of delivering the highest quality teaching and learning experience for the CEAS students, as well as advancing the scholarship of teaching and learning through a rigorous research enterprise. The department provides undergraduate students with tutoring, mentoring, advising and other support services, as well as valuable research opportunities. Graduate students learn about conducting fundamental research on engineering learning and bridging research and practice by defining effective practices, identifying the reasons why they work, developing curricula, assessing how students learn, and potentially moving those findings into future engineering classrooms. Faculty in the department teach fundamental courses in undergraduate engineering, as well as graduate courses in the discipline of engineering education. DECE is currently undertaking a significant restructuring of its first-year engineering program through support from the National Science Foundation. The University of Cincinnati is the birthplace of the cooperative education (co-op) program and CEAS holds the largest public mandatory cooperative education program at a public university in the United States. The CEAS total undergraduate and graduate enrollment is 7,000 students. Essential Functions Teach graduate and undergraduate courses in engineering education including, but not limited to, first-year engineering courses and courses designed to impart general technical and practical competencies to engineers across disciplines; Develop (or advance) a nationally/internationally recognized, externally-funded research program in engineering/CS education (evidenced by grantsmanship and/or published refereed journal/conference papers, commensurate with rank); Advise graduate students; Assist with ongoing maintenance and updating of the first-year engineering course sequence based on contemporary requirements and evidence-driven evaluation of outcomes. Perform service at the Department (e.g., student career mentoring, advising of student organizations, service on committees), College, University, and professional societies levels (including peer review for journals or funding agencies); Nurture collaborations across the college and campus; and work to achieve departmental, college, and university strategic goals; Further the department’s goals of developing and sustaining premier research programs and preparing students to become high-performing professionals in the workplace through collegial collaboration and teamwork within the Department of Engineering and Computing Education, with other CEAS departments, and other UC Colleges. Minimum Requirements Prior to the effective date of appointment, must hold a Ph.D. degree in Engineering Education, or a related discipline. Application Process Please submit applications online at http://jobs.uc.edu . Your application should include: Brief cover letter. Curriculum vitae. Teaching philosophy and plans for teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Research agenda including funding possibilities. Names, titles, and contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. Please use the Additional Documents feature in the application system to upload items 3) through 5) as separate files. FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104369 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Adjunct-Instructor-of-Chemistry%2C-UC-Clermont-College/104448-en_US": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor of Chemistry, UC Clermont College",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor of Chemistry, UC Clermont College Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Adjunct Instructor of Chemistry, UC Clermont College Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors You are invited to apply to be included in the general pool of candidates from which qualified faculty roles will be filled. The number of positions varies depending on the needs of the department and there is no guarantee that an open position currently exists. Appointments are renewed based on departmental need and teaching performance. Classes may be day or evening. Rank and compensation are based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. About UC Clermont UC Clermont College provides student-centered undergraduate education in an open-access, regional college environment. We foster diversity as well as intellectual, cultural, and social development in our community . UC Clermont College has an enrollment of approximately 3,500 students and is located in Batavia, Ohio, thirty miles east of the University of Cincinnati’s downtown campus.  We offer a variety of Associates degrees, two-year technical degrees, and certificates and also prepare students for transition/transfer into baccalaureate degree programs. UC Clermont’s open-access mission presents a unique opportunity for faculty with a strong commitment to provide a solid foundation for students at all levels to develop their academic skills and reach their educational goals. Teaching is assigned in 1 to 3 credit-hour sections; office hours are required in order to meet with students as needed.  Appointments are renewed based upon departmental need, enrollments, and teaching performance.  Rank and compensation are based on experience and academic credentials. Job Overview UC Clermont College is accepting applications for a part-time adjunct faculty for the upcoming spring semester.  Course(s) to be taught may include Chemistry major and non-major lectures and/or labs possibly including Organic Chemistry.  All courses are taught in-person and include both day and evening sections. Essential Functions Conduct and teach assigned undergraduate level courses on a part-time basis Perform other related duties as assigned Minimum Requirements Master's or PhD degree in Chemistry or related field required, with demonstrated ability or prior teaching experience. Required Experience Excellent written and oral communication skills with the ability to present complex material to students unfamiliar with a subject. Ability to work and collaborate with individuals from a variety of backgrounds. Additional Qualifications Considered Three (3)+ years of teaching experience in lecture and lab, both in-person and online.  Knowledge and experience with the Canvas Learning Management System. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. ( UC Benefits Link ) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE. Highlights include: Tuition Remission As a part-time employee, UC provides a limited tuition remission benefit based on role.  Tuition remission covers tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS). Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time – pro-rated by FTE.  Includes paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days). Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Professional development & mentorship opportunities University employee discount program To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104448 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/AssistantAssociate-Professor-of-OperaDirecting%2C-College-Conservatory-of-Music/104382-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Directing, College-Conservatory of Music",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Directing, College-Conservatory of Music Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant/Associate Professor of Opera/Directing, College-Conservatory of Music Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The University of Cincinnati College-Conservatory of Music (CCM), a nationally prominent comprehensive college for the performing and media arts, seeks an experienced opera director and teacher for a full-time tenure-track appointment, intended to begin August 15, 2027. Rank and salary will be commensurate with credentials.  Initial appointment is for three (3) years, after which the faculty member is eligible for reappointment. About the Opera Department CCM’s Opera department is a highly collaborative, multi-dimensional area of the Division of Theatre Arts, Production, and Arts Administration (TAPAA). The department offers Artist Diplomas in Singing, Coaching, and Stage Directing and also collaborates closely with the MM and DMA degrees in Vocal Performance. The Opera program provides training in the art and craft of opera through one of the most comprehensive programs available anywhere in the world. Productions are supported by the CCM’s Department of Theatre Design and Production, which offers degrees at the BFA and MFA levels in costume design and technology; lighting design and technology; make-up and wig design; sound design and technology; stage design, properties, and scenic art; stage management; and technical production. The program allows students to create world-class performances and develop the skills necessary to move with confidence onto the professional stage. CCM values excellence in teaching, innovation in curriculum, and the ability to connect with a diverse group of students seeking to explore a range of opportunities and careers in a rapidly changing marketplace. CCM faculty are committed to recruiting, mentorship, career preparation, and student support. CCM encourages an inquisitive, collaborative spirit and expertise in a broad and diverse range of repertoire. The department is seeking to enhance its current offerings with candidates who have research or performance interests in a related field reflective of the current job market. This could include but is not limited to areas such as community engagement, music entrepreneurship, music leadership, music technology, pedagogy, chamber music, or non-Western/non-classical repertoire. Essential Functions Direct mainstage and/or studio theatre productions each year. Teach courses, workshops, and seminars, as needed and appropriate in areas including but not limited to characterization, movement, stage combat, career development, libretto study, and directing. Teach individual coaching sessions with master’s, artist diploma, and doctoral students. Mentor artist diploma directing students in their work on departmental productions. Recruit and retain students of exceptional promise. Develop curricula that support student success in the current job market. Participate in departmental activities including recruitment, auditions, committee work, season planning, and student advising. Maintain regional, national, and international visibility through professional performances, engagements, and/or research and creative activity. Serve the college and university community through activities such as committee membership, academic advising, and mentorship of students. Experience in complementary disciplines such as film, TV, voice-over, broadcasting , and career development is desirable. Minimum Requirements Prior to the effective date of the appointment, the successful candidate must have either: 1. A Master’s Degree in Music or an Artist’s Diploma in Music or Opera, in addition to a minimum of five (5) years of professional experience. Such experience must include working at institutions of higher learning or professional opera houses. OR 2. A Bachelor’s Degree in Music in addition to a minimum of 10 years of professional experience. Such experience must include post-baccalaureate work experience at an institution of higher learning or professional opera houses. All candidates must demonstrate proficiency in at least one (1) major operatic language, whether French, German, or Italian, and demonstrate the ability to work in the others. All candidates must demonstrate training in movement and/or stage combat. Additional Qualifications Considered A record of successful student recruitment An established network within the opera industry that supports student career placement Experience directing both standard repertoire and contemporary or newly commissioned works Physical Requirements/Work Environment Office environment/no specific unusual physical or environmental demands. Application Process Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit their materials electronically by accessing the University of Cincinnati’s Recruitment site https://jobs.uc.edu and searching for position 104382 . Applicants should submit a letter of application, a current CV, and a summary page with indicators of teaching experience, their students’ accomplishments (such as competition performance and employment) and/or professional experience, contact information for three professional references, and links to audio/video of current performances and teaching examples. We will not accept CDs or other forms of non-electronic media. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Salary Information: Assistant Professor, $67,000 - $73,000; Associate Professor, $73,000 - $80,000 Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104382 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/AssistantAssociateFull-Professor-%28Tenure-Tenure-Track%29%2C-Department-of-Computer-Science/104450-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (Tenure / Tenure Track), Department of Computer Science",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (Tenure / Tenure Track), Department of Computer Science Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor (Tenure / Tenure Track), Department of Computer Science Posting Start Date: 10/15/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. About The Department The University of Cincinnati is a major research university in an urban and wide-range community. The University’s research portfolio includes a broad range of activities spanning physical, social and life sciences, engineering, and medicine. This position will provide an opportunity to form collaborative relationships within the college, across the university, and with regional industry. UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service. We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The University of Cincinnati (UC) Department of Computer Science (CS) ( https://ceas.uc.edu/academics/departments/computer-science.html ) in the College of Engineering and Applied Science (CEAS) invites applications for multiple open-rank tenured or tenure-track faculty positions (Assistant, Associate or Full Professor). Rank and salary will be commensurate with experience. We welcome candidates across all areas of computer science, including artificial intelligence and machine learning; systems and security; databases, programming languages and software engineering; vision, robotics and human-computer interaction; and theoretical computer science. Essential Functions Teach graduate and undergraduate computer science courses, in traditional classroom and online settings.  Courses may include but are not limited to core undergraduate computer science courses, in areas such as such as algorithms and software engineering, and graduate courses in the faculty member’s area of research. Develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program, or, if hired at the Associate or Full Professor level, continue to lead an independent, established externally funded research program. Publish in professional journals and refereed conference proceedings. Advise and mentor graduate and undergraduate students. Contribute to curriculum and course development. Participate in departmental, college, and university service such as but not limited to committee membership and student recruitment. Contribute to the growth of the department through collaboration within CEAS, across the University, and with regional industry, including but not limited to joint research proposals with faculty in other departments and colleges, and research partnerships with industry. Faculty appointed at the Associate or Full Professor level will also mentor junior faculty and help shape the direction of the department. Minimum Requirements The following is required prior to the effective date of appointment: PhD in Computer Science, Computer Engineering or another closely related field. Appointment with tenure is contingent on review and approval through the University’s regular promotion and tenure procedures. Application Process Interested candidates should upload a curriculum vitae and a cover letter that indicates the rank for which they wish to be considered. Under the ‘Additional Documents’ section, please also upload: a research statement, a teaching statement, and the names and contact information for three references. Reference letters will be requested only at the final stage of the search. Consideration of applications will begin on November 15, 2026 and will continue until the positions are filled. The anticipated start date is August 2027. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104450 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Asst%2C-Assoc%2C-Full-Professor%2C-TT%2C-Audiology-&-Hearing-Science%2C-College-of-Allied-Health-Sciences/104399-en_US": {
      "title": "Asst, Assoc, Full Professor, TT, Audiology & Hearing Science, College of Allied Health Sciences",
      "description": "Asst, Assoc, Full Professor, TT, Audiology & Hearing Science, College of Allied Health Sciences Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Asst, Assoc, Full Professor, TT, Audiology & Hearing Science, College of Allied Health Sciences Posting Start Date: 10/14/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors You are invited to apply to be considered for one of multiple vacancies of the same position.  The number of positions varies depending on the needs of the department. Rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders at the University of Cincinnati is searching for two tenure-track faculty members in audiology who will contribute to the department’s research, teaching, and service missions. These are 9-month positions beginning in Fall 2027 with rank (assistant, associate, full professor) commensurate with experience. The Department seeks faculty with expertise in the following areas of audiology and hearing science: hearing anatomy and physiology, including peripheral and central auditory and vestibular systems; auditory and vestibular pharmacology, including mechanisms of ototoxicity and pharmacologic interventions for hearing and balance disorders; auditory processing disorder assessment and management; and habilitative audiology. We also welcome applicants with expertise in transdisciplinary areas relevant to hearing sciences, including community-based participatory research and/or translational research/implementation science. Essential Functions Faculty will be responsible for advancing an independent research agenda, teaching in relevant content areas, and providing service to the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders, the College of Allied Health Sciences, the University of Cincinnati, the broader community, and the field of audiology. Research activity includes but is not limited to: pursuit of external funding, student mentorship, and the dissemination of research findings. Teaching and service activities include but are not limited to: course development, primary instruction of in-person and/or online courses, participation in student recruitment/admission/retention, participation in program committee work, and other activities as assigned by the department chair. Collaborative Opportunities Faculty in the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders benefit from rich collaborative environments both within and beyond the University of Cincinnati. Ongoing partnerships include local leaders in healthcare and education, such as Cincinnati Public Schools, Cincinnati Children’s Hospital Medical Center, the Cincinnati Veterans Affairs Medical Center, and UC Health. Within the university, there are several collaborations with College of Medicine, College of Education, Criminal Justice, and Human Services including the Arlitt Child and Family Research Education Center, and the UC Speech and Hearing Clinic. These diverse collaborative networks support both research and clinical innovation across the lifespan. Minimum Requirements Prior to effective date of appointment, all of the following are required: PhD in Audiology or closely related field Demonstrated expertise in one or more area(s) of specialization in audiology Evidence of an emerging or established independent research agenda, as demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications and/or scholarly presentations Teaching experience as the primary instructor for at least one college-level course or documented formal training in pedagogy Additional Qualifications Considered Clinical degree in Audiology (i.e., M.A., Au.D.) Prior grant funding Demonstrated student mentorship experience Application Process CV/Resume Cover Letter describing qualifications and interest in the position Research Statement Teaching Philosophy Statement Team Culture Statement Contact information for three (3) references If the candidate has prior teaching experience, course evaluations will be required for an on-campus interview, if selected. Interested applicants can apply online at https://jobs.uc.edu . Please use the \"Additional Documents\" function to upload Research and Teaching Statements. Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Contact Information Inquiries about the position should be directed to the Search Committee Co-Chairs, Dr. Jennifer Vannest (vannesjr@ucmail.uc.edu) and Dr. Katie Russell (russeke@ucmail.uc.edu). Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104399 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
