    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/AssociateFull-Professor-of-Greek-Archaeology%2C-Classics%2C-College-of-Arts-and-Sciences/104236-en_US": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor of Greek Archaeology, Classics, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Associate/Full Professor of Greek Archaeology, Classics, College of Arts and Sciences Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Associate/Full Professor of Greek Archaeology, Classics, College of Arts and Sciences Posting Start Date: 10/14/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The Department of Classics at the University of Cincinnati invites applications from tenure-track or tenured candidates for the Carl. W. Blegen Professor of Greek Archaeology at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. Candidates will be considered in any field of Aegean Prehistory and in any geographical area that complements the expertise of our current faculty, but are expected to have a record of ongoing scholarly research and publication. The position is endowed and comes with a competitive salary commensurate with candidate experience. In addition, the Semple Fund will support research and related travel. The Department of Classics offers a B.A. program in Classics, with concentrations in Classical Languages and Classical Cultures, and M.A. and Ph.D. programs in Bronze Age Archaeology, Classical Archaeology, Greek and Latin Language and Literature, and Ancient History. The faculty thrive in an interdisciplinary environment that educates students in archaeology, philology, and ancient history. The department is housed together with the Burnam Classics Library, one of the largest and best collections of resources in Classics in the world, including a collection of Byzantine and Modern Greek resources. The Department publishes Nestor , the bibliography for Aegean Prehistory, the journal Greek, Roman, and Byzantine Studies , and oversees the International Aegean conferences and Aegaeum series. The Department added the Archive of the Program in Aegean Scripts and Prehistory (PASP) in 2024, which contains documents from the careers of Emmett Bennet, Jr., Michael Ventris, and Alice Kober among other scholars of Linear B. Departmental Archives also include records from Carl Blegen’s excavations in Pylos and Troy. Full information about the department and its Archives can be found on our website: http://classics.uc.edu . Essential Functions Leverage the resources of the Blegen Chair and Semple Fund to enhance the research profile of the Department through research, publication, and fieldwork. Design and implement original research in their specialty area of Aegean Prehistory Direct or co-direct fieldwork projects in the Mediterranean Publish and disseminate research in peer-reviewed articles, chapters, or books, and contribute to final publications of archaeological field work projects Teach Greek archaeology courses at both undergraduate level such as “The Art and Archaeology of Ancient Greece,” “Palaces of the Bronze Age Aegean,” and graduate level such as seminars in their specialty area of Aegean Prehistory, and survey courses, “Prehistory 1” and “Prehistory 2.” Supervise graduate and undergraduate students on research projects, including Ph.D. dissertations. Participate in internal and external service activities such as editorial/peer review, career mentoring for students and junior faculty, and serving on departmental committees such as the Aegaeum committee or graduate studies committee. Minimum Requirements Prior to the effective date of appointment, all of the following are required: A doctorate in Classics, Archaeology, or a related field. Currently hold at least a tenure-track or tenured Associate Professor position. A record of ongoing scholarly research and publication in the field of Aegean Prehistory as evidenced by at least 10 peer-reviewed articles, book chapters, or essays (or any combination thereof) in addition to at least one of the following: a peer-reviewed scholarly book or a contribution of a single authored chapter or two chapters as co-author in the final publication of an archaeological project in the field of Aegean Prehistory Application Process Interested candidates should create an Applicant Profile and submit an online application at https://jobs.uc.edu/job/ Please upload: ·       A cover letter summarizing research and teaching interest. ·       A research statement (no more than 5 pages) describing future research interests, if appointed to the position. ·       A current curriculum vitae. In addition, the cover letter should include the names and email addresses of three persons who can provide confidential letters of reference. The committee will review applications beginning November 13, 2026, and conduct initial interviews by Zoom. The position will remain open until filled. UC will consider applications from qualified foreign national applicants requiring sponsorship. For questions, please email Classics@uc.edu with the subject line “Blegen Chair.\" Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104236 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Assistant-Professor%2C-Anthropology%2C-College-of-Arts-and-Sciences/104231-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Anthropology, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Anthropology, College of Arts and Sciences Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant Professor, Anthropology, College of Arts and Sciences Posting Start Date: 10/14/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The Department of Anthropology at the University of Cincinnati invites applications for a tenure track Assistant Professor in Sociocultural Anthropology. The position will conduct community-engaged research at the intersection of health and the environment and employ critical ethnographic and applied approaches to problem solving in urban or peri-urban communities. This hire will help build the department’s research profile in environmental and medical anthropology, and further the department’s commitment to applied, public, and engaged scholarship. The appointment is intended to begin August 15, 2027. Essential Functions Teach undergraduate and graduate-level disciplinary courses in the Department of Anthropology including but not limited to Introduction to Cultural Anthropology, Applied Anthropology, and upper division courses in the individual’s area of expertise at the intersection of health and the environment as well as community-engaged courses. Develop a scholarly and community-engaged research program focusing on the intersection of health and the environment. Sustain an active and promising research program consistent with disciplinary expectations at a Research 1 Institution, including publishing scholarly research and pursuing internal and external funding opportunities. Contribute to our undergraduate and graduate program through advising and mentoring students. Expand training opportunities for students in the areas of applied and community- engaged anthropology. Contribute service to the department, college, university, and the profession (such as but not limited to serving on a faculty or professional organization, committee participation or leadership, and peer-review of research proposals, journal articles, and manuscripts). Minimum Requirements Prior to effective date of appointment: ·      A PhD in Anthropology or a closely related field Application Process Interested and qualified applicants must complete our online application. In addition to completing the online application, please submit: A cover letter that explains how your education and qualifications align with the requirements and responsibilities outlined in the job ad A full and current CV A list of three references. (Letters of reference will be requested for short-list finalists.) UC is an Equal Opportunity Employer, who works to bring out the best in our students, faculty and staff to collaborate, create, innovate and compete in a global society. We are seeking individuals who want to be a part of a dynamic team. As part of a complete application, you will be asked to include a statement on how you define team and what makes a team function successfully. Please use the “additional documents” feature to include all these required items. Review of applications will begin Dec 1 , with applications accepted until the position is filled. Candidates can apply here https://jobs.uc.edu/ Questions should be directed to Dr. Daniel Murphy, Search Committee Chair at: murphdl@ucmail.uc.edu Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104231 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Assistant-Professor%2C-Asian-Studies-and-Religious-Studies%2C-College-of-Arts-and-Sciences/104199-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Asian Studies and Religious Studies, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Asian Studies and Religious Studies, College of Arts and Sciences Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant Professor, Asian Studies and Religious Studies, College of Arts and Sciences Posting Start Date: 10/14/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. About The Asian Studies Program The Asian Studies program in the College of Arts & Sciences (A&S) offers a B.A. degree, minor, and related certificate programs; its classes cover languages, humanities, and social sciences (see the program website ). The Religious Studies program offers an undergraduate certificate that provides a multidisciplinary approach to the study of religion (see the curriculum on the website ). Job Overview The College of Arts & Sciences at UC invites applications for the position of Assistant Professor in the area of Asian Religions. The candidate will have a joint appointment in the programs of Asian Studies and Religious Studies. Salary will be competitive and commensurate with the candidate’s qualifications. Essential Functions Teach four undergraduate courses each year in Asian Studies and Religious Studies, including but not limited to Religions of Asia; Introduction to the Study of Religion; and classes on Asian culture, society, and contemporary issues in the candidate’s area of specialization (for example, Chinese Culture, Korean Culture, Cultures of South Asia, Asian Film, etc.) Teach electives related to the candidate’s area of expertise Conduct and publish research in the candidate’s area of expertise Oversee undergraduate research and capstone projects Participate in departmental, College, and University service including (but not limited to) attending department meetings, serving on committees, and participating in shared governance Minimum Requirements Prior to the effective date of appointment, all of the following are required: PhD in Religious Studies with a focus on Asian Religions, Asian Studies with a focus on Religion, or a related field One academic year of experience teaching in a relevant field at the collegiate level as instructor of record Application Process Interested applicants should create an Applicant Profile and upload the following here: A CV A cover letter outlining the candidate’s qualifications for the position and ability to teach the required classes Teaching philosophy Contact information and email for three professional references, who may be contacted during the review process. Please use the Additional Documents option as needed for these documents. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. This position is H-1B eligible. For more information or with question please contact Jenny Caplan (caplanjr@ucmail.uc.edu), search committee chair. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104199 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
    },
    "https://jobs.uc.edu/job/Assistant-Professor%2C-Experimental-Physical-Chemistry%2C-College-of-Arts-and-Sciences/104235-en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Experimental Physical Chemistry, College of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Experimental Physical Chemistry, College of Arts and Sciences Job Details | University of Cincinnati Skip to main content Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Language English (United States) Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Sign In To My Profile Employee Login Menu Search Jobs Sign in or Create Account FAQ Why Choose UC Search Jobs Job Description Apply now Job Title: Assistant Professor, Experimental Physical Chemistry, College of Arts and Sciences Posting Start Date: 10/14/26 Work Arrangement: Onsite Current UC employees must apply internally via SuccessFactors Faculty rank and compensation will be based on experience and academic credentials. Next Lives at the University of Cincinnati Founded in 1819, the University of Cincinnati ranks among the nation’s best urban public research universities. Home to more than 53,600 students, 12,000 faculty and staff, and over 360,000 living alumni, UC, a Carnegie 1 institution, combines research prowess with a physical setting The New York Times has called “the most ambitious campus design program in the country.\" UC's momentum has never been stronger as the anchor of the Cincinnati Innovation District, the oldest cooperative education (co-op) program in the country with students earning $94 million annually through paid experiences, an academic health system, and as a member of the Big 12 Conference. The university contributes $10.6 billion in economic impact to the city and $22.7 billion to the state of Ohio. At UC, next is all of us. Learn more at uc.edu . UC is a mission-driven organization where we are committed to student success and positively transforming the community through scholarship and service.  We thrive on innovation, making an impact, and fostering an environment where staff and faculty are key contributors to UC’s success. Job Overview The Department of Chemistry in the College of Arts & Sciences at the University of Cincinnati seeks applications to fill a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in experimental physical chemistry, broadly defined. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to biophysical, physical organic, measurement science and materials, and other interdisciplinary areas of experimental chemistry. Essential Functions Teach both undergraduate and graduate courses, such as, but not limited to, introductory classes in Chemistry and advanced classes in areas of interest as noted above. Develop a vigorous, externally funded research program that addresses fundamental problems in chemistry using state-of-the-art experimental approaches. Research may incorporate emerging methodologies such as automation, high-throughput experimentation, advanced data analysis, machine learning, and/or artificial intelligence, as appropriate to the candidate’s research program. Advise graduate and undergraduate students. Participate in relevant service to the department, college, and university through active participation in various committees such as but not limited to service on doctoral student committees, graduate admissions, and recruiting. There will be an opportunity to collaborate on research in the candidate’s area of expertise across the university, including but not limited to the College of Arts and Sciences and the Colleges of Engineering and Medicine. Minimum Requirements Prior to effective date of appointment, a Ph.D. degree in Chemistry, or in a closely related field. Application Process In order to be considered for this position, applications must be submitted electronically via our online hiring system at https://jobs.uc.edu (search for requisition #104235). In addition to the online application, candidates should include: a cover letter defining interest in the position a current curriculum vitae statement of the proposed research program (3 pages), a statement of teaching plans (1 page), and contact information (telephone and e-mail) for three references. We will begin reviewing applications November 9th and continue until the position is filled. Proposed Start Date: August 15, 2027 or as negotiated. Compensation and Benefits UC offers an exceptional benefits package designed to support your well-being, financial security, and work-life balance. (UC Benefits) Eligibility may vary by position and FTE status. Highlights include: Salary Information: Comprehensive Tuition Remission UC provides tuition remission for you and your eligible dependents, covering tuition costs for nearly all undergraduate and graduate programs offered by the university. Robust Retirement Plans As a UC employee, you won’t contribute to Social Security (except Medicare). Instead, you’ll choose between state pension plans (OPERS, STRS) or an Alternative Retirement Plan (ARP), with UC contributing 14–18% of your salary based on position. Real Work-Life Balance UC prioritizes work-life balance with a generous time-off policy, including: Vacation and sick time 11 paid holidays and additional end-of-year paid time off (Winter Season Days) 6 weeks of paid parental leave for new parents Additional Benefits Include: Competitive salary based on experience Comprehensive health coverage (medical, dental, vision, prescription) Flexible spending accounts & wellness programs Professional development & mentorship opportunities To learn more about why UC is a great place to work, please visit our Careers Page . UC is an E-Verify employer.  If hired into this position, you will be required to provide satisfactory proof of employment eligibility by providing acceptable, original forms of identification for employment verification via the Federal I-9 employment verification process. Click here for a list of acceptable documents. Important : To apply you must create a profile and submit a complete job application through the UC applicant portal . We are unable to consider “easy apply” applications submitted via other websites. For questions about the UC recruiting process or to request accommodations with the application, please contact UC HR at jobs@uc.edu . **FOR ALL FACULTY HIRES OFFICIAL ACADEMIC TRANSCRIPTS WILL BE REQUIRED AT THE TIME OF HIRE Equal Opportunity Employer.  Building a workplace where all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment, including Individuals with Disabilities and Protected Veterans. REQ: 104235 Apply now Clery and HEOA Notice Notice of Non-Discrimination eAccessibility Concern Privacy Statement University of Cincinnati | 2600 Clifton Ave. | Cincinnati, OH 45221 | ph: 513-556-6000"
